Name of the Teacher : Priti Sawale
Department : Political Science
Identified Major Gaps (Summary)
1.	Conceptual Gap – Nuclear diplomacy seen only as weapons, not diplomacy
2.	Analytical Gap – Limited evaluation of strategic and diplomatic consequences
3.	Application Gap – Poor linkage between theory and real-world events
4.	Terminology Gap – Confusion over treaties and doctrines
________________________________________
Strategies to Bridge the Gaps
Teaching Strategies
•	Use timelines (Pokhran tests → India–US Nuclear Deal)
•	Explain policies using simple language and diagrams
•	Include case studies (2008 Civil Nuclear Agreement)
Learning Strategies
•	Group discussions on “Is nuclear diplomacy a strength or risk for India?”
•	Short note writing on treaties and doctrines
•	Map-based learning for geopolitical context
Assessment Strategies
•	Structured answer templates (Introduction–Body–Conclusion)
•	Assertion–Reason and case-based questions
•	Regular formative quizzes
________________________________________
 Expected Outcomes After Gap Bridging
Students will be able to:
•	Clearly explain nuclear diplomacy in Indian politics
•	Analyze India’s nuclear policy and global position
•	Evaluate India’s stance on international nuclear treaties
•	Write well-structured analytical answers in exams
________________________________________
Conclusion
The gap analysis highlights that while students possess basic factual knowledge, there is a need to strengthen conceptual clarity, analytical skills, and application-based understanding of nuclear diplomacy in Indian politics. Targeted teaching strategies can effectively bridge these gaps and improve learning outcomes.
